WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.450
In the scene where Dylan Tucker is securing&nbsp;
his family during the Purge, we see him use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.560
an app based security interface. The UI is clearly&nbsp;
the same as the interface of the Sandin Security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.520
system seen back in the first movie. There have&nbsp;
been many references to Sandin Security since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.180
then. The company is never mentioned by name&nbsp;
here, but it’s clearly supposed to be the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.200
system. To learn some of the interesting&nbsp;
secrets hidden around The Yeehaw Purge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.040
or as you may know it, The Forever Purge,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.670
(METAL MUSIC)

00:00:28.919 --> 00:00:30.300
It’s&nbsp;the Purge!

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.274
 The Yeehaw Purge! I&nbsp;
still can’t believe they didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:36.660
call it that. Huge missed op. The Forever&nbsp;
Purge doesn’t even make that much sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.620
Most of the characters in the movie call it&nbsp;
The Ever After Purge. Not The Forever Purge.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.880
So THE YEEHAW PURGE was written by series&nbsp;
creator James Demonaco and directed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.560
Everardo Valerio Gout, who had mostly worked in&nbsp;
TV. His previous film was an award winner at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.680
Mexican Academy of Film, so he was probably&nbsp;
seen as a good choice to be able to direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.760
a movie with scenes that contain English and&nbsp;
Spanish interchangeably. The use of language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.620
actually relates to one of our Things You Missed,&nbsp;
so let’s get in -- to the cosas que te perdiste.

00:01:01.868 --> 00:01:07.190
(YEEHAW rodeo music)

00:01:07.526 --> 00:01:10.260
Early in the movie, we see the border&nbsp;
wall between the US and Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.860
which now blocks citizens from being able to&nbsp;
see into the other country, unlike the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.880
wall which has slits. This is probably done to&nbsp;
increase the feeling of division and tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.380
between the two nations. It goes along with the&nbsp;
graffiti seen later: Build bridges, not walls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.340
It also allows people to deface the wall with&nbsp;
graffiti, some of which is kind of impressive.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.960
This American flag has the hammer and sickle&nbsp;
from the old USSR flag, suggesting that someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.400
wants to see the US transition to communism. The&nbsp;
government in power during the Purge movies is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.140
the NFFA, or New Founding Fathers of America. They&nbsp;
are portrayed to be objectively evil, but they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.520
clearly not the type of evil that subscribes&nbsp;
to communism. For example, the NFFA thrives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.240
on division between different levels of income,&nbsp;
while communism looks to eliminate the free market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.380
entirely. So this symbol suggests the emergence&nbsp;
of a new group, which we see before long.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.520
The opening titles show animated scenes of&nbsp;
the USA, with each becoming corrupted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.200
red splatters and scary imagery. One of these&nbsp;
shows a good ol’ fashioned American barbecue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.020
and the guests become corrupted&nbsp;
with purge masks, one of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.060
looks just like Kimmy’s mask&nbsp;
from The Purge Election Year.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.360
Also, there’s one that I find funny.&nbsp;
We see this street front with shops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.880
like Victoria’s Bakery and Kyle’s Dress Boutique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.421
but when it glitches out it just becomes Kyle’s&nbsp;
DIE. Now that sounds like somewhere I would shop.

00:02:16.421 --> 00:02:20.160
We learn that the NFFA has been voted back&nbsp;
into power again. And this is where I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.720
to pause the analysis for a little rant. The&nbsp;
last movie chronologically before The Yeehaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.140
Purge was The Purge Election Year, where we&nbsp;
saw Senator Charlie Roan win the election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.340
thus ending the NFFA’s reign of power.&nbsp;
But at the very end, there’s an uprising.

00:02:33.158 --> 00:02:37.020
– [TV announcer] We’re just now hearing reports about&nbsp;
a few scattered incidents around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:41.340
country where NFFA supporters are&nbsp;
reacting violently to this defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.500
they are burning cars, breaking windows,&nbsp;
looting, attacking police officers --

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.640
– And then it just ends, the movie&nbsp;
ends. Then they did a prequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.780
then they did two seasons of the TV series,&nbsp;
which both took place before Election Year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.500
So all of this time, we’re waiting to see&nbsp;
what happened after the election. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.159
they announce The…

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:01.579
The Yeehaw Purge!

00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:03.240
And&nbsp;
it looks like it’s going to be about groups who

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.960
refused to stop purging. I thought it was gonna&nbsp;
take place right after Election Year. But it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.300
not. It’s years later. President Roan would have&nbsp;
been in power from 2040 to 2044. In the US you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.520
can serve a maximum of two 4-year terms, so if&nbsp;
she was re-elected, she’d last until 2048. Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.140
only real clue about the date in The Yeehaw Purge&nbsp;
is this poster for a Purge Week BBQ on Friday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.300
March 19th. It doesn’t matter if Roan served one&nbsp;
or two terms, the first time March 19th lands on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:33.360
a Friday is the year 2049. Yes, The Purge has now&nbsp;
caught up to Blade Runner and San Francisco Rush.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.140
This is what future tech looks like in&nbsp;
Blade Runner 2049. Here’s what it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.704
like in The Purge 2049.

00:03:38.704 --> 00:03:40.171
Transportation in&nbsp;
Blade Runner.

00:03:40.171 --> 00:03:41.820
Transportation in Purge. I’m

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.980
sure the real 2049 will fall somewhere in-between,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.160
but still, we’re getting scammed out&nbsp;
of potentially cool sci-fi elements.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.520
Anyway, in America we ended up not&nbsp;
having to wait for a Purge movie to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.220
show us what it looks like when people&nbsp;
refuse to accept losing an election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.280
and that is as much time as&nbsp;
I’m willing to spend on that.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.940
Our main characters this time are Juan and&nbsp;
Adela, who are illegal immigrants looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.240
to get away from the drug cartel in their&nbsp;
home country. We see Adela making a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.900
effort to learn English. Everything in their&nbsp;
house is labeled with sticky notes containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.180
both the English and Spanish words. Sink,&nbsp;
dish, curtains, toaster, flower pot, plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.260
television, and others. She scolds Juan for&nbsp;
not putting in the same effort that she does…

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.220
– [Adela] Please, where are we? You&nbsp;
need to practice your English!

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.540
– And she listens to an audio program that seems&nbsp;
to teach her what to say if she ever gets caught.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.800
– [audio program] Repeat after me. I was just visiting friends in Texas.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.700
– She’s even seen with a book called English&nbsp;
Fast Track Learning For Spanish Speakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.000
though it would make more sense if&nbsp;
the title of the book was in Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.080
considering the target audience wouldn’t&nbsp;
understand English until after reading the book…&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.800
The reason I bring all of this up is because&nbsp;
the language barrier comes into play at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.520
when Adela is captured and forced to call&nbsp;
her people for help in order to lure them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.540
into a trap. She calls out in Spanish, and he&nbsp;
demands she speak English so he can understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.860
but she refuses. As a result, her friends&nbsp;
are able to save her. Usually in a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.800
the main character goes through a&nbsp;
character arc, where a character is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.820
one way at the beginning and undergoes a change&nbsp;
that allows him or her to overcome an obstacle.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.000
In The Purge, James Sandin learns&nbsp;
that a stranger’s life is just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.060
valuable as his family’s and gives&nbsp;
him a chance to live. In Anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.300
Leo Barnes realizes that revenge on&nbsp;
his son’s killer will not make him feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.260
better about the loss. Adela almost has like&nbsp;
a reverse-character arc, because she basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.920
goes back to the way she was before&nbsp;
the movie. It feels kind of backwards.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.580
Dylan has more of a traditional character&nbsp;
arc. At the beginning wants his culture to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.420
stay separate from Mexican culture, and he’s&nbsp;
envious of Juan’s cowboy skills. At the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.440
he has to work together with Juan and he finds&nbsp;
that he is now the one crossing the border and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.820
integrating into Mexican society. In that sense,&nbsp;
he’s actually a lot like Leo Barnes in Anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.540
who started off wanting to be a lone hunter,&nbsp;
but ended up being saved by his new friends.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.520
There are a lot of loose homages to previous&nbsp;
Purge movies. One example is Chiago Harjo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.360
Early on we see him on TV preaching the&nbsp;
truth about how the Purge is not effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.080
at releasing anger. Later on he meets with the&nbsp;
main characters and helps escort them through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.340
danger. He’s filling the role of Carmelo Johns&nbsp;
in Anarchy, or Dante Bishop in Election Year;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.780
the revolutionary that our characters see on&nbsp;
TV and later meetup with to receive assistance.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.380
By the way, his broadcast about how the&nbsp;
Purge actually only increases anger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.160
and does not decrease it, is an echo of&nbsp;
the research done by Professor Drew Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.640
in The Purge: Season 2. Chiago doesn’t&nbsp;
specifically mention Professor Adams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.140
but we can assume he is referencing her study,&nbsp;
which the NFFA unsuccessfully tried to cover up.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.320
Like in past Purges, there is a lot&nbsp;
more we can learn from looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.560
some of the small details hidden in the&nbsp;
graffiti and artwork around the city.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.797
(impact)

00:06:27.576 --> 00:06:31.621
(mysterious music)

00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:35.520
The immigrant character Adela works in a butcher&nbsp;
shop. You may have thought about how this feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.180
representative of The Purge, which temporarily&nbsp;
turns the entire country into a butcher shop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.560
In fact, the logo features two knives coming&nbsp;
together, different, but also reminiscent of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.060
clash of knives seen on the official NFFA seal&nbsp;
which first appeared in Anarchy. This idea is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.140
further touched on at the beginning of The Purge.&nbsp;
As everyone locks down we cut from the ranch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.040
where all the cattle are herded together in&nbsp;
the dark, to the passengers on the bus full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.660
potential purge victims. It’s very symbolic of how&nbsp;
human lives are treated like cattle in this world.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:03.540
The bus, by the way, says Lemercier Public Schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.900
on the side. It’s named after one of&nbsp;
the producers, Sebastien K Lemercier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.020
and this isn’t the first time there’s&nbsp;
been a Purge easter egg to reference him.

00:07:10.224 --> 00:07:15.636
– This right here, is an original Lemercier.

00:07:15.636 --> 00:07:19.560
– Overall, the NFFA takes a back seat this&nbsp;
time thanks to the rise of the radical group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.040
The Real Patriots of America. I’ve talked&nbsp;
a lot in my Purge videos about how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.580
NFFA is always associated with the color&nbsp;
blue, which matches the color of the blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.300
baptisias. In the world of The Purge, people&nbsp;
put these flowers out to show support for The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.860
Purge. We only see one example in this movie,&nbsp;
maybe a sign that the NFFA is losing favor.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:39.420
On the contrary, the New Patriots of America&nbsp;
logo is red, they probably made that choice so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:44.481
they can be viewed as enemies of the NFFA. I also&nbsp;
noticed that the emergency broadcast screen is red&nbsp;again.

00:07:44.481 --> 00:07:48.420
They have also reinstated immunity for government&nbsp;
officials ranking 10. Guess they didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.860
like their President getting killed too much in&nbsp;
Election Year and decided to roll that one back.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.360
We don’t see much of the blue baptisias this time,&nbsp;
but there’s a new flower of importance: the red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.920
roses. Our characters are told to follow the roses&nbsp;
if they get into trouble in America. Being red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.120
they symbolize safety, whereas the blue flowers&nbsp;
symbolize violence. I’ve talked before about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.500
this is the opposite of what you’d expect, which&nbsp;
is probably why the NFFA chose blue, to deceive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.860
people. These can be seen throughout the city.&nbsp;
It’s established that they guide our characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.000
to safety, but what isn’t immediately obvious&nbsp;
is how directions are hidden in each painting.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.660
This one is part of a mural with a one way&nbsp;
sign, telling them to go right. The next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.380
one has a grim reaper pointing to the&nbsp;
left. This poster shows a Lucha Libre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.160
wrestler pointing up and to the right.&nbsp;
The stem of this rose extends into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.820
arrow pointing left. All of these signals&nbsp;
lead them to the neon rose at the end.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.540
There are other relevant pieces of street&nbsp;
art. When everyone arrives at the safe zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.260
I noticed artwork of a giant pair of eyes&nbsp;
with a gunshot in between. The Purge TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.800
series featured lots of eye graffiti&nbsp;
as a symbol of the NFFA’s dystopian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.580
surveillance. This area is essentially the new&nbsp;
underground triage center from Election Year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.060
The intercutting between it and the more&nbsp;
comfortable conditions of the ranch family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.020
relaxing at home show how The Purge affects&nbsp;
different social classes much more than others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.720
an idea that has been heavy in the&nbsp;
series ever since the first movie.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.640
It seems the Florida man meme is one of the many&nbsp;
things that hasn’t changed by 2049. The line that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.540
the broadcaster leaves us with before going&nbsp;
off the air is also a significant reference.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.940
– [broadcaster] Good night, Texas. And good luck.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.180
– [Zac] It’s a nod to the 2005 movie,&nbsp;
Good Night and Good Luck.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.980
– Good night, and good luck.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:18.180
– Yeah, that’s the one. I talked earlier about&nbsp;
the communist symbol appearing on the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.780
flag on the border wall. Good Night and Good&nbsp;
Luck is all about a group of journalists who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:25.320
try to expose the truth about the American&nbsp;
politician Joseph McCarthy, who was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.100
reckless and unsubstantiated accusations of&nbsp;
communism around the time of the Cold War.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:32.940
The trailer for Good Night and Good Luck describes&nbsp;
America during this period as a “a nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:37.320
terrorized by its own government”, certainly, not&nbsp;
a far cry from what’s going on in The Purge, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.700
under very different circumstances. Good Night and&nbsp;
Good Luck deals with the integrity of the media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.900
which is not really an aspect of this particular&nbsp;
Purge film. There’s no journalist character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:50.520
the movie. The only somewhat relevant beat is when&nbsp;
the NFFA announces that anyone displaying the Ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.360
After Purge flag will be arrested immediately,&nbsp;
which of course would be unconstitutional in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.320
the United States, because it would violate&nbsp;
freedom of speech. A lot like how McCarthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.280
tried to silence his critics. The journalism&nbsp;
aspect might be relevant if the filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:05.460
are trying to give themselves a pat on the back;&nbsp;
essentially saying that they are making a brave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.240
statement through their art. The last few Purge&nbsp;
films have gotten more and more pointed towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.920
aspects of the real life USA political situation.&nbsp;
Again, not really interested in getting into that.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.600
So let’s talk about franchise callbacks. As this&nbsp;
Purge begins, we see scenes of purging happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.560
around the city, as we often do. Here there&nbsp;
are a bunch of men tracking victims with dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.380
as seen during Anarchy. This&nbsp;
dude gets dragged behind a motorcycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:30.120
something we saw in The First Purge. Then there’s&nbsp;
this firing squad set up right outside a church.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.840
The firearms go off right as the church&nbsp;
bells strike midnight. This is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.380
of Midnight Purge Mass, which we were first&nbsp;
introduced to in The Purge: Election Year.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.740
The saddest part about 2049 is not&nbsp;
the Purge, but the fact that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.729
still don’t have protected bike&nbsp;
lanes. Get it together America!

00:10:43.729 --> 00:10:47.460
Later on there’s also an encounter with this guy&nbsp;
with a swastika on his face, as kind of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.480
spiritual successor to Earl Danzinger, the&nbsp;
neo-nazi from Election Year. He refers to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:55.320
gunshot sounds all around them as “homegrown&nbsp;
music from the heartland.” I think he’s meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:59.580
to signify the attitude of some gun enthusiasts;&nbsp;
the idea that it becomes a recreational activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:03.420
like listening to music, and they may lose sight&nbsp;
of how dangerous these weapons actually are.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.980
We meet this guy inside the police van which is&nbsp;
rounding up Ever After Purgers. In typical Purge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:11.580
fashion, this van gets rolled over. That’s&nbsp;
basically tradition at this point in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:16.140
franchise, it happened in Election Year and in&nbsp;
Season 2. One tradition they seemed not to uphold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.380
however, was having a character named Sabien.&nbsp;
Then there’s this:

00:11:21.264 --> 00:11:23.220
(horsey jump scare)

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:28.680
Yes, they really did try to make a jumpscare out&nbsp;
of literally just a horse walking. LOL. Then we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.560
got my favorite guy. His name’s Kirk. The Yeehaw&nbsp;
Purge! Then there are some not so great guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.640
like this man in the high vis vest. Not&nbsp;
exactly great for sneaking around during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.540
Purge. He also uses a mallet as his weapon,&nbsp;
even though it seems very easy to get guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.440
in this world. Guess this dude’s playing&nbsp;
on hard mode. As were the motorcyclists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:49.320
who thought they could challenge a big&nbsp;
truck. Not a battle you’re gonna win.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.800
The characters realize their best bet&nbsp;
is to seek asylum by crossing the border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.760
back into Mexico. I’m not gonna talk about the&nbsp;
Dracula, Frankenstein and Psycho appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:01.380
because they’re not even references. They’re&nbsp;
just there. But 99K USD is a damn good deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:06.300
for a luxury condo, especially considering&nbsp;
inflation by 2049. What a deal. I wonder if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:10.680
can walk from there to the festival in Central&nbsp;
Park. Better check website.com to find out.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.340
One of the things I like to do is scan&nbsp;
all the graffiti in the Purge films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.040
see if there’s anything interesting&nbsp;
written. In my video on Anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.820
I talked about how they took a shot at Hollywood&nbsp;
film producer David Neustatder, by making a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.420
named David Neustatder be one of the Purge&nbsp;
victims found in the street. In this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:28.680
there’s a message that reads: Kill David Dead.&nbsp;
There’s probably no connection… but if there is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.100
wow! They’re still not over this&nbsp;
are they? The mystery deepens.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.520
Anyway, the Native American Chiago is&nbsp;
gonna help them get across the border,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:39.060
but not before we stumble upon&nbsp;
a few more eye opening details.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:43.322
(impact)

00:12:44.205 --> 00:12:47.673
(mysterious music)

00:12:47.673 --> 00:12:52.140
An interesting detail about the finale is that&nbsp;
The Forever Purgers don’t need masks anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.560
but a couple of them continue to wear them&nbsp;
anyway. I think it’s generally accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:59.100
that the purpose of the masks is to hide the&nbsp;
Purgers’ identities, because after The Purge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:02.880
you gotta go back to work, and even people&nbsp;
that participated are ashamed. But with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:06.600
the Ever After Purge, they aren’t planning&nbsp;
on ever going back to normal. Perhaps some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.660
were confident that it really would&nbsp;
last forever, while others were not.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:12.300
But they do have these hats though.&nbsp;
How much you want to bet these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.040
the same hats that they gave the cast and crew?

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.900
We hear a new chant from the Purgers.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:18.660
– Purge and Purify, ever after.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:19.759
– [crowd] EVER AFTER!

00:13:19.759 --> 00:13:23.340
– Actually, it’s more of a twist on an old&nbsp;
classic. We first heard chants of Purge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:27.900
and Purify in Election Year. These anti-NFFA&nbsp;
radicals have taken a motto from the NFFA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.500
and twisted it into something of their own. I&nbsp;
think it’s meant to be a parallel to groups that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.160
latch onto one political idea and fester&nbsp;
into their own little cult, or whatever.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:40.080
The battle rages on. This guy thinks he’s in a&nbsp;
Legend of Zelda game with the bomb arrows. As our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.821
characters get closer to the border, the purgers&nbsp;
close in on them. There’s a Wilhelm Scream.

00:13:44.201 --> 00:13:44.701
(scream)

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.140
If you aren’t familiar, the Wilhelm Scream&nbsp;
is a funny sound effect found in many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.040
many movies, usually when a character falls&nbsp;
down or gets shot or something. It’s named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:56.220
after a character named Private&nbsp;
Wilhelm from the 1953 Western film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.740
The Charge At Feather River,&nbsp;
which used it over and over.

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:02.226
(screams, over and over)

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:06.960
You can find compilations on YouTube that show&nbsp;
just how many movies, shows and games have sampled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.040
this sound. So I was absolutely shocked that&nbsp;
this is the first Wilhelm Scream I’ve pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.640
out on Things You Missed. I guess it kind of&nbsp;
makes sense… it’s a very silly sound effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.600
so it’s probably not that likely to be used&nbsp;
in horror movies. This is a horror channel.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.720
I guess it’s fitting that the sound&nbsp;
became famous in a Western, and I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:25.680
introducing it in this Purge movie with Western&nbsp;
elements. We should start a Wilhelm counter.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:26.880
(Wilhelm scream)

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:30.840
We’ll see if we ever revisit that. Anyway,&nbsp;
the characters make it across the border,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.300
and Dylan and Cassie are able&nbsp;
to safely have their baby.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.000
– It’s a girl. Do you want to hold your daughter?

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:40.500
– [Zac] I’m sorry, WHAT? What the **** is going on&nbsp;
here? Do they not know how this works? Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.200
they just run out of pink hats?&nbsp;
How do you run out of pink hats?

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.540
This was supposed to be the&nbsp;
last movie in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.900
but it answers nothing and it really&nbsp;
just raises more questions. In fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.600
the first thing we hear during&nbsp;
the credits is “this isn’t over.”

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.640
– What will the new America look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.040
when this all ends?
– ♪ This isn’t over! ♪

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:00.660
– [Zac] It wasn’t long before James Demonaco suddenly had&nbsp;
an idea for a new Purge movie, and reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.800
it went into development. It’s pretty clear now&nbsp;
that we were lied to about The Yeehaw Purge being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:09.360
the last movie, but honestly, I’m kind of glad.&nbsp;
This next one will allegedly bring Frank Grillo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.320
back. He’s the guy that plays Leo Barnes. That’s&nbsp;
something that I wanted for this movie, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.400
wouldn’t be right to close things out without a&nbsp;
proper conclusion to the Leo Barnes trilogy. I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:21.960
also like to see the adult Charlie Sandin, the kid&nbsp;
from the first one. He’d be in his 40s now. He’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.220
character who lived most of his life in The Purge&nbsp;
era, lost his father in The Purge at a young age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:30.420
and has been shown to be selfless the last time&nbsp;
we met him. He could be a useful character to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:35.220
to as the country continues to fall apart.&nbsp;
As for villains, Joe Owens and Godhead 2.0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:39.900
from the TV series are, as far as we know, still&nbsp;
alive. They are among the most powerful villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:43.980
we’ve ever seen and they could easily be side&nbsp;
villains that work alongside one final threat.

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:48.360
I am probably hyping this up too much, we’ll&nbsp;
probably JUST get Frank Grillo, but when The Purge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:53.640
returns so will I. With another episode of Things&nbsp;
You Missed. Click the playlist on the left for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:58.920
of my Purge analysis’ and remember to subscribe&nbsp;
